      "text": "To solve the system of equations\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11 \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwe can use the method of elimination or substitution. Here, we will use the elimination method.\n\nFirst, we will eliminate one of the variables by making the coefficients of \\(y\\) in both equations equal in magnitude but opposite in sign. To do this, we can multiply the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 5:\n\n\\[\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11) \\implies 6x - 10y = -22\n\\]\n\\[\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12) \\implies 35x + 10y = -60\n\\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n6x - 10y = -22 \\\\\n35x + 10y = -60\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNext, we add the two equations together to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60)\n\\]\n\\[\n6x + 35x - 10y + 10y = -82\n\\]\n\\[\n41x = -82\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(x = -2\\), we substitute this value back into one of the original equations to solve for \\(y\\). We will use the first equation:\n\\[\n3x - 5y = -11\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(x = -2\\):\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5y = -11\n\\]\n\\[\n-6 - 5y = -11\n\\]\nAdding 6 to both sides:\n\\[\n-5y = -5\n\\]\nDividing by -5:\n\\[\ny = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\((x, y) = (-2, 1)\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}\n\\]",
